# What the source distribution carries, stated explicitly.
#
# Why this file exists at all, what it fixed, and how to verify it offline:
# docs/packaging.md. What follows is only what a reader of these patterns needs.
#
# The grants below are by directory, not by file extension. A grant written as
# `tests/*.py` would omit the first fixture that is not Python, and an artifact
# test that filtered its expectation the same way would omit it too and stay
# green: that is the defect this file was written for, reappearing one extension
# over. Granting the directory fails in the other direction instead, loudly, by
# putting an unwanted file somewhere a test can see it.
#
# The consequence is that whatever lives in a granted directory ships, so the
# residue patterns at the bottom are not decoration. They are the half of the
# design that the grants depend on.
#
# The patterns are matched against the filesystem, not against git. Two durable
# reasons, neither of which expires: an sdist has to be buildable from an export
# carrying no .git at all, which is the normal state of a distribution build
# root, and deriving contents from version control would impose a build-time VCS
# dependency on every downstream packager. That several test modules and two of
# the documents happen to be uncommitted right now is a corroborating example,
# not the argument.

include LICENSE
include README.md
include pyproject.toml

# tests/ ships so the suite can be run against the artifact by whoever received
# it, which includes the three modules beside the tests that are not tests:
# conftest.py, target_mod.py and integrity_corpus.py. docs/ ships because the
# doc tests read and execute those documents, resolving them relative to the
# archive root and failing on a missing file rather than skipping. examples/
# ships because a source distribution of a fault injection toolkit that omits
# its worked faults is missing part of its source.
recursive-include tests *
recursive-include docs *
recursive-include examples *

# Residue, removed after the grants rather than pre-filtered into them, which is
# what lets the grants stay as wide as they are. The suffixes cover what a test
# run, a compiler and an editor leave behind in a directory that ships whole,
# and the two backup patterns cover what an overwrite leaves behind.
#
# Two of those rather than the one that appears to do the same job. These
# patterns are free at the start of the path and anchored at its END, so `*.bak`
# matches notes.bak and misses notes.bak.1789617984, and the second shape is the
# only one this tree has ever produced, from `cp file file.bak.$(date +%s)`. The
# single `*.bak*` catches both and also sweeps notes.bakery.md, which has to
# keep shipping: a wanted file dropped from a release is the same defect as an
# unwanted one added, minus the symptom. Measured against the compiled patterns
# rather than read off the format documentation, in docs/packaging.md.
#
# Order matters and it is the reason this line sits here rather than at the top:
# these patterns sweep the grants ABOVE them and nothing below. A new
# recursive-include appended to the end of this file, which is the natural place
# to add one, ships its bytecode and its editor backups. New grants go above
# this line.
global-exclude *.py[cod] *.so *.log *.swp *~ .DS_Store *.bak *.bak.*

# Directories no grant above reaches, pruned anyway. They remove nothing today,
# and the reason to keep them is not symmetry: publishing is irreversible, the
# tracker holds internal notes, and a later pattern widened to the repository
# root would sweep it into a release. An unnoticed redundant prune costs a line.
# A leaked tracker cannot be withdrawn once it is on an index.
prune backlog
prune .github
prune .claude
prune .pytest_cache
